# .spool/tree.req

feature: TREE

requirements:

  - id: TREE-1
    title: Invariants in the Requirements Tree
    description: >
      The Requirements view lists each requirement's invariants beside
      its acceptance criteria, shows whether each one is tested, opens
      its definition when clicked, and counts invariants in the
      requirement's and feature's summaries.
    status: active
    deciders:
      - Matt Ames
    consulted: []
    date: 2026-11-06
    rationale: >
      Spool tracks invariants like ACs (INVARIANT-1) and the language
      server sends them in the spool/traceability tree (INVARIANT-2-7),
      but the Requirements view only reads "acs", so invariants are
      invisible in the sidebar and an untested one doesn't show up
      where untested ACs do. Invariants are listed after the ACs, as in
      the .req file, rather than under a group of their own, so the
      tree keeps three levels. Summaries count them separately from
      ACs, as Spool's reports do, so an existing project's view doesn't
      change until it has invariants.
    superseded_by:
    out_of_scope:
      - Grouping, filtering or sorting the tree
      - Changes to the language server or its spool/traceability response
      - Specifying the existing AC, requirement and feature behavior beyond
        what invariants change
      - Which requirements a feature's summary counts; it counts the same
        ones it counts today
    acceptance_criteria:

      - id: TREE-1-1
        title: Invariants Listed Under Their Requirement
        description: >
          Given a requirement with ACs and invariants in the
          spool/traceability tree,
          when the requirement is expanded,
          then its children are its ACs followed by its invariants, each
          in the order the tree lists them, and each invariant is
          labeled "<ID>: <title>".

      - id: TREE-1-2
        title: Invariant Test Status
        description: >
          Given an invariant with a testCount,
          when it is shown in the tree,
          then it shows "<n> test(s)" when tested and "untested"
          otherwise, with the same icons and colors an AC uses for each;
          the -INV- in its label is what tells it apart from an AC.

      - id: TREE-1-3
        title: Invariant Opens Its Definition
        description: >
          Given an invariant with a file and a line,
          when it is clicked in the tree,
          then the .req file opens at the invariant's line.

      - id: TREE-1-4
        title: Requirement Summary Counts Invariants
        description: >
          Given an active requirement,
          when it is shown in the tree,
          then a requirement without invariants keeps today's
          "<tested>/<total> tested" summary of its ACs, and one with
          invariants also shows "<tested>/<total> invariants" for them;
          a requirement that isn't active shows its status, as today.

      - id: TREE-1-5
        title: Feature Summary Counts Invariants
        description: >
          Given a feature,
          when it is shown in the tree,
          then a feature whose requirements have no invariants keeps
          today's summary, and one with invariants also shows
          "<tested>/<total> invariants" summed over the same
          requirements its AC summary counts.

      - id: TREE-1-6
        title: Older Server Without Invariants
        description: >
          Given a spool/traceability tree whose requirements have no
          "invariants" field, as an older Spool sends,
          when the tree is shown,
          then it shows the ACs and summaries as before, with no
          invariants and no error.
